<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Boole</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Boole</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Boole was an English mathematician and philosopher, born in Lincoln in 1815, who transformed logic from a philosophical study of language into a mathematical system of algebraic equations. The son of a shoemaker, he was largely self-taught, began teaching at 16 and ran his own schools before becoming in 1849 the first professor of mathematics at Queen’s College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>. His Mathematical Analysis of Logic and The Laws of Thought (1854) set out Boolean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, which Claude Shannon and Victor Shestakov later applied to relay circuits, leading to digital computers. He died in 1864 after developing pneumonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-boole.jpg" alt="George Boole" width="330" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_George_Boole.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boole was the son of John Boole, a shoemaker, and Mary Ann Joyce. He had a primary school education and some lessons from his father, but a serious decline in the family business left him little formal teaching. The Lincoln bookseller William Brooke may have helped with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and he taught himself modern languages. At 16 he became the breadwinner for his parents and three younger siblings, with a junior teaching post at Heigham's School in Doncaster.</p><p>Through the Lincoln Mechanics' Institute Edward Bromhead helped him with mathematics books. Without a teacher it took him many years to master calculus. At 19 he opened a school in Lincoln, and in 1840 he returned there to run a boarding school. He took part in the campaign for early closing and, with Edmund Larken and others, set up a building society in 1847.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His memoir of 1844, &quot;On a General Method in Analysis&quot;, won the first gold prize for mathematics awarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>. His interest in logic was prompted by a debate between Sir William Hamilton and Augustus De Morgan. In 1847 he published The Mathematical Analysis of Logic, which he later regarded as a flawed exposition. Contrary to widespread belief he never meant to disagree with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s logic but to systematise it and widen its range.</p><p>In 1849 he became the first professor of mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rutgers-university">Queen's College</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, where he met Mary Everest in 1850; they married in 1855. He won the Keith Medal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> in 1855 and was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in 1857.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late November 1864 Boole walked three miles in heavy rain to the university and lectured in wet clothes. He fell ill with pneumonia, and as his wife believed that remedies should resemble their cause, she wrapped him in wet blankets. He died on 8 December 1864.</p><p>William Stanley Jevons and Charles Sanders Peirce extended his work. In 1937 Claude Shannon showed in a master's thesis that Boolean algebra could optimise electromechanical relay systems. Victor Shestakov proposed a similar theory in 1935 but first published it in 1941. University College Cork marked the 200th anniversary of his birth in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Boole” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
